@@ -0,0 +1,102 @@
+# ESP32-4848S040 functional RGB display and touch
+
+The `esp32-4848s040` system profile connects the existing ESP32-S3 CPU, GPIO,
+LCD_CAM, GDMA and I2C models to an ST7701 480 × 480 RGB panel and a GT911
+single-contact touch controller. Firmware drives the devices through their
+registers. The host receives display artifacts and supplies touch inputs
+through the existing generic simulation interfaces.
+
+This is a functional model. It does not establish physical board equivalence,
+calibrated display timing, Arduino/LVGL compatibility, or a production release.
+
+## Modeled behavior
+
+- LCD_CAM RGB565 output consumes guest-memory GDMA descriptors and pixel bytes.
+  The active width and height set the frame boundary. A complete frame raises
+  VSYNC; automatic next-frame mode supports circular descriptor chains.
+- DMA handles odd byte splits, byte/bit order, descriptor ownership and optional
+  owner writeback. Invalid, inaccessible, CPU-owned or malformed descriptors
+  report a descriptor error. Short or absent chains do not complete a frame.
+- ST7701 receives the 9-bit GPIO control stream. DCS reset, sleep, display state
+  and backlight determine the visible framebuffer. Extended command-bank
+  parameters are retained, without analog voltage or gamma simulation.
+- GT911 provides a 16-bit I2C register pointer, identification, configuration
+  storage, ready/count state and one contact. A report remains stable after the
+  guest observes it until the guest writes zero to acknowledge it. Later motion
+  coalesces into the next report. Held-contact refresh uses a fixed 10 ms interval.
+- Existing LCD_CAM i80 behavior uses its original transfer path.
+
+Pixel clock, porches, RGB pin multiplexing and analog panel behavior are outside
+this model. VSYNC represents completed DMA active-area data. GT911 reset/address
+strapping, IRQ delivery, gestures, multiple contacts and EEPROM persistence are
+not modeled. The configured I2C route records the board wiring; it does not
+qualify wrong-pin NACK behavior. Samples and test results do not qualify an
+arbitrary vendor firmware image.
+
+## Wiring and generic interfaces
+
+| Connection | Profile value |
+| --- | --- |
+| ST7701 control CS / SCK / MOSI | GPIO39 / GPIO48 / GPIO47 |
+| Backlight | GPIO38, active high |
+| Touch SDA / SCL | GPIO19 / GPIO45 |
+| GT911 address | `0x5d` |
+| Touch reset and IRQ | Not connected in this polling profile |
+
+The display artifact has ID `display`, kind `framebuffer`, format `rgb565_be`,
+and width/height 480. Metadata exposes `display_on`, `sleeping`, `backlight_on`,
+`powered` and `frames_received`. A dark screen can therefore be distinguished
+from firmware that has not submitted a complete frame.
+
+Touch has component ID `touch`. The existing generic input channels are `x`
+and `y` (0–479 pixels) and `pressed` (0 or 1). Set coordinates before pressing,
+preferably together through `set_inputs`. Change coordinates while pressed for
+motion; set `pressed` to zero for release. This port adds no device-specific
+MCP tool or external GPIO-drive API.
+
+## Reproducible software checks
+
+The complete original Xtensa guest source is
+`tests/fixtures/esp32-4848s040/build_probe.py`. It uses Python's standard library
+to emit real instructions and an ELF. The result is 18,032 bytes, with SHA-256
+`fc5563c16da88dc609c5ada36b17c72a611cc4998675d502572318c1657c419f`.
+
+The guest configures GPIO control, starts RGB DMA, polls VSYNC, reads and
+acknowledges GT911 reports over I2C, and submits a new frame for down/move/up.
+Its first frame has four distinct corner colors. Touch changes pixel zero to
+the X coordinate read by firmware; release restores red. No host framebuffer
+setter paints this image. UART emits `S040 READY` and `S040 TOUCH`.
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/fixtures/esp32-4848s040/build_probe.py
+cargo test -p labwired-core --test esp32s3_rgb_s040 --test esp32s3_lcd_i80_pixels
+cargo test -p labwired-core --features event-scheduler --test esp32s3_rgb_s040 --test esp32s3_lcd_i80_pixels
+cargo test -p labwired-core --lib peripherals::components::gt911
+node tests/fixtures/esp32-4848s040/check_wasm.cjs /path/to/nodejs-engine
+```
+
+The fixture README documents the physical SRAM1 layout and independent source
+references. A separate integration test checks MMU-mapped octal PSRAM reads.
+The Node check records the exact WASM and JavaScript hashes. These checks are
+software qualification; the existing release process and hardware validation
+requirements remain separate.
+
+## Provenance
+
+This is a selective MIT-licensed port from
+[CrispStrobe commit 1843597](https://github.com/CrispStrobe/labwired-core/commit/184359726288d048ef315d4fa4e99f1f737208a0),
+with the generic RGB attachment seam from
+[b627fafc](https://github.com/CrispStrobe/labwired-core/commit/b627fafc4e7bd9d853708b1a62495d7a32fe963b)
+and model registrations from
+[0efad509](https://github.com/CrispStrobe/labwired-core/commit/0efad50949f8ab6c8e008d07b913ad363fd5658c).
+The reviewed source snapshot is `d543a46bba894f9e7e3aa31cdba6feb561b2d08e`.
+Unrelated fork model changes, performance baselines and hardware drift
+acknowledgements are excluded.
+
+Primary implementation references:
+
+- [Arduino_GFX board definition](https://github.com/moononournation/Arduino_GFX/blob/6da54cb7f7ce6fde1ef8435186eb60406ea4caa0/examples/PDQgraphicstest/Arduino_GFX_dev_device.h)
+  and [ST7701 type9 initialization](https://github.com/moononournation/Arduino_GFX/blob/6da54cb7f7ce6fde1ef8435186eb60406ea4caa0/src/display/Arduino_RGB_Display.h).
+- [ESP-IDF 4.4.7 LCD registers](https://github.com/espressif/esp-idf/blob/v4.4.7/components/soc/esp32s3/include/soc/lcd_cam_reg.h)
+  and [RGB driver](https://github.com/espressif/esp-idf/blob/v4.4.7/components/esp_lcd/src/esp_lcd_rgb_panel.c).
+- [Espressif GT911 driver](https://github.com/espressif/esp-bsp/blob/67f41a169401d1f23e827188dd08fb351cb0febe/components/lcd_touch/esp_lcd_touch_gt911/esp_lcd_touch_gt911.c).
